      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>
As discussed, your house has quite a bit of excess air leakage that is coming from a few key areas:

** There is a low wall return grate in the hallway near the living room that allows ALOT of cold air into the house (as noticed when I performed the blower door guided air leakage test). The one floor grate in the master bedroom (right by the door into the bedroom) is the same way ....... with alot of air leakage. It is almost as if these two openings are connected to the same ductwork and iit is somehow open into the crawlspace ?? For comparison, none of the heating floor vents around the perimeter walls had much air leakage at all.

** Along the bottom of the exterior entrance doors to your house - especially the one at the back of the house.

** There is a hole under the kitchen sink adjoining the bathtub in the adjacent bathroom. There is a heater under there to keep pipes from freezing, however the root cause is air leakage from the cold crawlspace below, so this areas needs to be air sealed and better insulated.

** The attic hatch can be sealed with a removeable caulking, such as Draft Attack.

** The woodstove draws its' combustion air from inside the house and therefore allows some air leakage through the combustion air opening at the bottom of the back of this woodstove. Rather than drawing your warm inside air to burn &amp; send up the chimney, I recommend you install an outside combustion air supply pipe directly to the back of this woodstove and have it sealed to the stove.

Savings: There should be a minimum 19% reduction in overall air leakage achieved from completing the above air sealing that could potentially save $ 211 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 5.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 715 in rebates from the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative. Based on the current high level of air leakage in your house, I would estimate that you can exceed this 19% target by another 20% - 30% !

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $715 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on achieving the target outlined above. This grant can increase up to $1,300 based on how much you exceed the target.

An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.
</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>
The exterior walls of your house are 2x4@16" with R12 insulation. As most new housing has a minimum of R30 walls, I thought I would model adding another 3" (R15) of rigid extruded polystyrene foam board insulation to the exterior of the house ........ including the estimated energy savings &amp; possible grants/rebates to help you decide.

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). Most homeowners participating in this program are already committed to (or seriously considering) an exterior siding + new windows retrofit ............ so our rebate is meant to help cover some of the cost of the additional insulation to go behind that new siding.

Savings:
By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R15, you could potentially save $ 769 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 18.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 5,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). 

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

PLEASE NOTE: the DHERP is scheduled to end on January 31, 2024, so you would only have this summer to complete this upgrade &amp; qualify for the $5,000. Please let us know ASAP if you plan to do this, so we can pre-approve you for funding.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $4,940 is available for adding R15 insulation to 100% of the exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <windows />
                <doors>
Your house has 2 exterior entrance doors and they both have some air leakage around them or along the bottom door sweep. You may be able to have new weather-stripping installed this summer, or perhaps you may choose to replace them with new ENERGY STAR® qualified units. If you replace them, I recommend doors with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/m²K or an Energy Rating of a minimum 34.

Savings:
By replacing these 2 entry door systems with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 16 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 0.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $325.00 in rebates (2 door units @ $162.50 each) through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative.

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $162.50 per hinged door system (includes all windows in the door, sidelights and transom windows above) is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>
Your house currently has a 184 litre electric hot water tank. As electricity is quite expensive in Norman Wells and this electric hot water tank is essentially a large "kettle" in your house, I have modeled the opportunity to replace the hot water tank with a direct-vent, oil-fired semi-on-demand hot water heater with an energy factor of 0.70 or higher (eg: Toyotomi OM-122DW)

Savings: By replacing your current electric hot water tank with a direct-vent, oil-fired semi-on-demand hot water heater with an energy factor of 0.70 or higher, could potentially save $ 675 per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by 4.1 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
 
To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>
Your house is currently heated with a very old 82.1% efficient propane furnace. I have modeled an option for you to upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 96% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat.

Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 96% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $ 783 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 21.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil- or gas-fired furnace or boiler with an ENERGY STAR certified natural gas- or propane-fired furnace or boiler that is a minimum 97% AFUE. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>
Renewable Energy Systems

The Arctic Energy Alliance administers the Alternative Energy Technologies Program (AETP) that includes rebates for Solar PV systems, including integrated battery energy storage for NWT residents who are not connected to the hydro-electric grid. 

Savings: As a starting point for your further investigation, I modeled a 4 kW PV system, flush mounted on the rear facing roof (South), directly connected to the Norman Wells grid (ie: not including a battery storage system). This is estimated to produce about 2,998 kWh annually, reducing your electricity bill by $ 980, and qualifying for up to $20,000 from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technology Program.

For more information and pre-approval rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
https://aea.nt.ca/program/renewable-energy/

NOTE: The GNWT Alternative Energy Technology Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

Canada Greener Homes Grant 

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a solar photovoltaic system technology that converts sunlight directly into electricity. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.

Batteries Connected to Photovoltaic Systems to Provide Standby Power for Home

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1300 is available for the installation of battery storage and an inverter to connect to a photovoltaic system to provide standby power for the home for an existing PV system or a new install. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes Grant in order to be eligible. This grant may be combined with the new PV system installation grant for a maximum grant of $5000. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>
CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.

Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.

ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION

For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.

Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:

? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report
? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.
? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit
? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional
? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:
     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*
     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards
     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home
*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.
? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.

ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES

You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.

Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources. Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) and the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:
https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Wood Stove Heat - Circulated by your furnace fan !!

The Arctic Energy Alliance assigns a nominal cost of $100/cord for self-harvested wood (amortized purchase &amp; use of a chainsaw + the safety gear and transportation costs). I ran a quick calculation to see how much you would save (compared to using your current 92.9% efficient oil furnace) if you used your current wood stove for 50% of the annual heating of your home.

I also mentioned an opportunity for you to have your heating contractor set up your thermostat so the furnace circulation fan could operate based upon a programmed "upper temp" setting to recirculate the heat from your woodstove down through the floor of your house &amp; all the way out to the bedrooms at far end. This would keep a more even heat in your house and help protect water and drain lines from freezing !!

Your current wood stove is 82.5% efficient and is not currently direct-vented (ie: having its' own dedicated outside combustion air supply directly connected/sealed to the back of the wood stove), however I highly recommend you have one installed. The annual net heating cost savings to use this wood stove for 50% of the heating load of your house is estimated to be $2,678 per year.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Norman Wells, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year)  from t he GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the range hood &amp; the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 0.4 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The current wood stove is chimney vented, without a dedicated &amp; sealed combustion air supply, and can be susceptible to having combustion products back-drafted into the house. The furnace is drawing its' combustion air from inside the house, however it power vents its' exhaust to the outside &amp; a power vented appliance usually will not back-draft until a pressure difference of about 15 - 25 Pascals is achieved.

During my assessment of your home, I did see smoke detectors, but no carbon monoxide (CO) detectors? Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following ventilation:
        - Kitchen range hood exhaust fan
        - There is no bathroom exhaust fan
        - Clothes dryer

If you choose to replace or install any exhaust fans, please select ENERGY STAR® qualified units. For a new bathroom fans, you should consider installing timer switches (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so they will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs - Norman Wells

The following Nov. 1, 2022 values, before GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Norman Wells:
Electricity - $18.00 min, $0.31 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.54 per kWh thereafter
Oil  - $2.17 per litre 
Wood - $100 per cord self-harvested, $800 per cord purchased
Pellets - $10.00 per bag, $660.00 per tonne bulk delivered

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Disclaimer
The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Your Energy Advisor

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.

Sincerely,

Ken Baigent, Contracted Senior Energy Management Specialist
Arctic Energy Alliance
Phone: (867) 920-3333
Fax: (867) 873-0303
E-mail: k.baigent@aea.nt.ca
Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

